    <!-- ===== True X-ray and skeleton facts ===== -->
    <string name="fact_hand_bones">Each human hand has 27 bones: 8 in the wrist, 5 in the palm and 14 in the fingers.</string>
    <string name="fact_pelvis">A female pelvis is usually wider and rounder than a male pelvis, with a larger opening that helps in childbirth.</string>
    <string name="fact_white_bones">Bones look white on an X-ray because the calcium in them absorbs more X-rays than soft tissue does.</string>
    <string name="fact_bone_count">An adult skeleton has about 206 bones. A newborn baby has around 270, and many of them fuse together while growing up.</string>
    <string name="fact_wavelength">X-rays are a kind of light we cannot see. Their wavelength is about 0.01 to 10 nanometres, far shorter than visible light.</string>
    <string name="fact_roentgen">Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895. One of the first X-ray photos showed his wife\'s hand, wearing her ring.</string>
    <string name="fact_nobel">Röntgen won the very first Nobel Prize in Physics, in 1901, for discovering X-rays.</string>
    <string name="fact_stapes">The smallest bone in your body is the stapes, inside the middle ear. It is only about 3 mm long.</string>
    <string name="fact_femur">The femur (thigh bone) is the longest and strongest bone in the human body.</string>
